<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adamawa Emirate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1809">1809</a>–<a href="/years/1901">1901</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Fombina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Adamawa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fula">Fulani</a> state founded around 1809 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modibbo-adama">Modibbo Adama</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usman-dan-fodio">Usman dan Fodio</a>, as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a>. Centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yola">Yola</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benue-river">Benue River</a>, it expanded over the highlands of what are now north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>. In 1901 it was divided between British Nigeria and German Kamerun, and the Lamido of Adamawa still reigns at Yola.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adamawa-emirate.jpg" alt="Adamawa Emirate" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 7bdIa9 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adamawa_State_Flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Muhammad Bello and brother Abdullahi dan Fodio led the campaigns against Gobir, while flagbearers such as Sulaimanu in Kano, Musa in Zaria and Modibbo Adama in <b>Adamawa</b> carved out new emirates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It consisted of two separate areas: the Northern Cameroons, bordering Bornu and <b>Adamawa</b>, and the Southern Cameroons, with Buea as its centre.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-cameroons">British Cameroons</a></li></ul></section>
